# Roth & Wehrle - Nutrition & Diet Therapy.
# Chapter list: 12th edition (2018), from the publisher's table of contents on cengage.com.
# Summaries cover each chapter's standard topics; they are written for this site, not copied from the book.

C = [
    (1, "The Relationship of Nutrition and Health", [
        "Nutrition is the result of the processes by which the body takes in and uses food. Good nutrition supports growth, energy, healthy body weight, resistance to disease, and long life. The six classes of nutrients are carbohydrates, fats (lipids), proteins, vitamins, minerals, and water. Energy-yielding nutrients are carbohydrates (4 kcal/g), proteins (4 kcal/g), and fats (9 kcal/g).",
        "Malnutrition means bad nutrition and includes both undernutrition and overnutrition. Deficiency diseases develop when a nutrient is lacking, such as scurvy (vitamin C), rickets (vitamin D), and kwashiorkor (protein). Infants, older adults, pregnant people, people with chronic illness, and those with low income are at higher risk. Nutrition assessment uses diet history, anthropometric measurements (height, weight, BMI), clinical exams, and lab tests.",
    ], [
        "Six nutrient classes: carbohydrates, lipids, proteins, vitamins, minerals, water",
        "Carbs and protein 4 kcal/g; fat 9 kcal/g",
        "Malnutrition = undernutrition or overnutrition",
        "Nutrition assessment: diet history, anthropometrics, clinical exam, labs",
    ]),
    (2, "Planning a Healthy Diet", [
        "The Dietary Guidelines for Americans recommend a healthy eating pattern across the life span: variety, nutrient-dense foods, limiting added sugars and saturated fats to under 10% of calories each, and reducing sodium to under 2,300 mg a day. MyPlate shows the five food groups: fruits, vegetables, grains (half whole grains), protein foods, and dairy.",
        "Food labels list serving size, calories, nutrients, and percent Daily Values (5% or less is low, 20% or more is high). Health claims and terms like 'low fat' and 'reduced sodium' are regulated by the FDA. Cultural and religious food customs, such as kosher, halal, and vegetarian patterns, influence diets and should be respected in diet planning.",
    ], [
        "Added sugars and saturated fats under 10% of calories",
        "Sodium under 2,300 mg/day",
        "MyPlate: fruits, vegetables, grains, protein, dairy",
        "%DV: 5% low, 20% high",
    ]),
    (3, "Digestion, Absorption, and Metabolism", [
        "Digestion breaks food into simple nutrients. Mechanical digestion (chewing, churning, peristalsis) and chemical digestion (enzymes) work together. Digestion begins in the mouth with salivary amylase, continues in the stomach with hydrochloric acid and pepsin, and is mostly completed in the small intestine with pancreatic enzymes and bile (which emulsifies fats).",
        "Absorption moves nutrients through the walls of the small intestine, which are lined with villi that increase surface area. The large intestine absorbs water and forms feces. Metabolism is all the chemical changes nutrients undergo: anabolism builds tissues and catabolism breaks them down to release energy. Basal metabolic rate (BMR) is the energy needed for basic body functions at rest.",
    ], [
        "Digestion starts in the mouth; most happens in the small intestine",
        "Bile emulsifies fat; pancreatic enzymes digest all three macronutrients",
        "Villi increase absorptive surface area",
        "Anabolism builds; catabolism breaks down",
    ]),
    (4, "Carbohydrates", [
        "Carbohydrates are the body's main source of energy. Simple carbohydrates (sugars) include monosaccharides (glucose, fructose, galactose) and disaccharides (sucrose, lactose, maltose). Complex carbohydrates (polysaccharides) include starch, glycogen (stored in liver and muscle), and fiber.",
        "Fiber is not digested. Soluble fiber (oats, beans, fruit) can lower cholesterol and blood sugar; insoluble fiber (whole wheat, vegetables) adds bulk and prevents constipation. Adults need about 25–38 g of fiber a day. Lactose intolerance results from a lack of the enzyme lactase. Too much added sugar contributes to tooth decay and weight gain.",
    ], [
        "Main energy source; glucose is the body's fuel",
        "Glycogen = stored carbohydrate in liver and muscle",
        "Soluble fiber lowers cholesterol; insoluble adds bulk",
        "Lactose intolerance = lack of lactase",
    ]),
    (5, "Lipids (Fats)", [
        "Lipids provide concentrated energy (9 kcal/g), carry fat-soluble vitamins, insulate the body, and protect organs. Most dietary fats are triglycerides. Saturated fats (solid at room temperature, from animal foods and tropical oils) raise LDL cholesterol. Unsaturated fats (liquid, from plant oils, nuts, and fish) include monounsaturated and polyunsaturated fats such as omega-3 fatty acids.",
        "Trans fats, formed by hydrogenation, raise LDL and lower HDL and are now largely removed from the U.S. food supply. Cholesterol is made by the liver and found only in animal foods. LDL ('bad') carries cholesterol to tissues and can build up in arteries; HDL ('good') carries it back to the liver.",
    ], [
        "Fat = 9 kcal/g; carries vitamins A, D, E, K",
        "Saturated = solid, raises LDL",
        "Omega-3 fats in fish, flaxseed, walnuts",
        "LDL 'bad'; HDL 'good'",
    ]),
    (6, "Proteins", [
        "Proteins build and repair tissue, form enzymes, hormones, and antibodies, help balance fluids, and can provide energy (4 kcal/g). They are made of amino acids; the nine essential amino acids must come from food. Complete proteins (meat, fish, eggs, dairy, soy) contain all essential amino acids; incomplete proteins (most plant foods) lack one or more but can be combined (complementary proteins).",
        "Adults need about 0.8 g of protein per kg of body weight. Protein-energy malnutrition includes kwashiorkor (protein deficiency with edema) and marasmus (severe calorie and protein deficiency causing wasting). Excess protein is stored as fat and stresses the kidneys.",
    ], [
        "Made of amino acids; 9 are essential",
        "Complete proteins: animal foods and soy",
        "RDA ≈ 0.8 g/kg body weight",
        "Kwashiorkor = protein deficiency; marasmus = overall starvation",
    ]),
    (7, "Vitamins", [
        "Vitamins are organic compounds needed in small amounts to regulate body processes. Fat-soluble vitamins (A, D, E, K) are stored in the body and can become toxic in excess. Vitamin A supports vision (deficiency causes night blindness); D helps absorb calcium (deficiency causes rickets and osteomalacia); E is an antioxidant; K is needed for blood clotting.",
        "Water-soluble vitamins (B complex and C) are not stored in large amounts and must be eaten regularly. Thiamin (B1) deficiency causes beriberi; niacin (B3) deficiency causes pellagra; folate prevents neural tube defects; B12 deficiency causes pernicious anemia; vitamin C deficiency causes scurvy. Supplements can help some people but should not replace food.",
    ], [
        "Fat-soluble: A, D, E, K (stored, can be toxic)",
        "A night blindness; D rickets; K clotting",
        "B1 beriberi; B3 pellagra; B12 pernicious anemia; C scurvy",
        "Folate prevents neural tube defects",
    ]),
    (8, "Minerals", [
        "Minerals are inorganic elements that build bones and teeth and regulate body processes. Major minerals (needed in amounts over 100 mg/day) include calcium, phosphorus, magnesium, sodium, potassium, and chloride. Trace minerals include iron, iodine, zinc, selenium, copper, and fluoride.",
        "Calcium builds bones and teeth; deficiency contributes to osteoporosis. Sodium and potassium maintain fluid balance; excess sodium raises blood pressure. Iron forms hemoglobin; deficiency causes iron-deficiency anemia. Iodine is needed for thyroid hormone; deficiency causes goiter. Fluoride strengthens teeth against decay.",
    ], [
        "Major minerals > 100 mg/day; trace minerals less",
        "Calcium: bones; deficiency → osteoporosis",
        "Iron: hemoglobin; deficiency → anemia",
        "Iodine: thyroid; deficiency → goiter",
    ]),
    (9, "Water", [
        "Water makes up about 50–60% of body weight and is the most essential nutrient: a person can survive only a few days without it. It transports nutrients and wastes, regulates body temperature, lubricates joints, and is part of chemical reactions.",
        "Water comes from beverages, foods, and metabolism, and leaves through urine, sweat, breath, and feces. Fluid balance depends on electrolytes like sodium and potassium. Dehydration causes thirst, dark urine, dizziness, and confusion; older adults and infants are at higher risk. Edema is excess fluid in tissues.",
    ], [
        "Water ≈ 50–60% of body weight",
        "Most essential nutrient",
        "Electrolytes maintain fluid balance",
        "Dehydration: thirst, dark urine, dizziness",
    ]),
    (10, "Nutrition during Pregnancy and Lactation", [
        "Pregnancy increases needs for calories (mostly in the second and third trimesters), protein, folate, iron, calcium, and iodine. Folic acid before and during early pregnancy prevents neural tube defects such as spina bifida. Recommended weight gain depends on pre-pregnancy BMI (about 25–35 pounds for normal weight).",
        "Pregnant people should avoid alcohol (fetal alcohol spectrum disorders), smoking, raw or undercooked meat and fish, high-mercury fish, and unpasteurized foods (listeria risk). Common concerns include nausea, heartburn, constipation, gestational diabetes, and preeclampsia. Lactation increases calorie and fluid needs; breast milk is the ideal infant food.",
    ], [
        "Folic acid prevents neural tube defects",
        "Normal-weight gain ≈ 25–35 lb",
        "No alcohol; avoid high-mercury fish and unpasteurized foods",
        "Lactation needs extra calories and fluid",
    ]),
    (11, "Nutrition During Infancy", [
        "Infants grow faster than at any other time; birth weight usually doubles by about 4–6 months and triples by 1 year. Breast milk is recommended as the sole food for about the first 6 months; it provides antibodies and is easy to digest. Iron-fortified formula is the alternative.",
        "Solid foods begin around 6 months, one new food at a time to watch for allergies, starting with iron-rich foods. Honey should not be given before 1 year (botulism risk), and cow's milk should not replace breast milk or formula before 1 year. Choking hazards such as whole grapes and nuts must be avoided.",
    ], [
        "Birth weight triples by 1 year",
        "Breast milk alone for about 6 months",
        "Start solids ~6 months, one at a time",
        "No honey or cow's milk before age 1",
    ]),
    (12, "Nutrition during Childhood and Adolescence", [
        "Children grow steadily and need nutrient-dense foods, regular meals and snacks, and appropriate portion sizes. Picky eating is common; offering new foods repeatedly helps. Childhood obesity is a major concern linked to sugary drinks, screen time, and inactivity.",
        "Adolescents have a growth spurt and need more calories, calcium (to build peak bone mass), and iron (especially girls after menstruation begins). Concerns include skipping meals, fast food, sports nutrition myths, and eating disorders such as anorexia nervosa and bulimia nervosa.",
    ], [
        "Children need nutrient-dense foods and regular snacks",
        "Childhood obesity linked to sugary drinks and inactivity",
        "Teens need more calcium and iron",
        "Eating disorders often begin in adolescence",
    ]),
    (13, "Nutrition during Adulthood", [
        "Adults' calorie needs decrease with age as metabolism slows and activity often drops, but nutrient needs stay the same or increase. A healthy diet and exercise help prevent chronic diseases such as heart disease, diabetes, and cancer.",
        "Older adults may need more calcium, vitamin D, and vitamin B12 (absorption decreases with age). Problems that affect nutrition include poor dentition, reduced sense of taste and smell, decreased thirst, limited income, isolation, and medications that interact with food. Programs like Meals on Wheels help.",
    ], [
        "Calorie needs drop with age; nutrient needs don't",
        "Older adults need more calcium, vitamin D, B12",
        "Decreased thirst raises dehydration risk",
        "Social and economic factors affect older adults' nutrition",
    ]),
    (14, "Weight Management across the Life Cycle", [
        "Body mass index (BMI) = weight (kg) ÷ height (m)². A BMI of 18.5–24.9 is healthy, 25–29.9 overweight, and 30 or more obese. Waist circumference shows abdominal fat, which raises disease risk. Obesity increases the risk of type 2 diabetes, heart disease, hypertension, and some cancers.",
        "Weight loss requires a calorie deficit: about 3,500 calories equals 1 pound of fat. Safe loss is about 1–2 pounds per week through balanced diet, physical activity, and behavior change. Fad diets rarely work long term. Being underweight also carries risks and requires increasing calorie intake.",
    ], [
        "BMI = kg ÷ m²; 18.5–24.9 healthy; 30+ obese",
        "≈3,500 kcal = 1 lb fat",
        "Safe loss: 1–2 lb per week",
        "Combine diet, exercise, behavior change",
    ]),
    (15, "Diet, Pre-diabetes and Diabetes", [
        "Diabetes mellitus is a disorder of carbohydrate metabolism caused by too little insulin or the body's resistance to it. Type 1 is autoimmune and requires insulin; type 2, the most common, is linked to obesity and inactivity; gestational diabetes occurs during pregnancy. Pre-diabetes means blood sugar is higher than normal but not yet diabetic.",
        "Medical nutrition therapy uses consistent carbohydrate intake, carbohydrate counting or exchange lists, high fiber, and limited added sugars. Hypoglycemia (low blood sugar) is treated with 15 g of fast-acting carbohydrate. Complications include heart disease, kidney disease, neuropathy, and retinopathy. The A1C test shows average blood glucose over about 3 months.",
    ], [
        "Type 1 = no insulin; type 2 = insulin resistance",
        "Pre-diabetes = elevated but below diabetes range",
        "Treat hypoglycemia with 15 g fast carbs",
        "A1C = 3-month glucose average",
    ]),
    (16, "Diet and Cardiovascular Disease", [
        "Atherosclerosis, the buildup of plaque in arteries, causes most heart attacks and strokes. Risk factors include high LDL cholesterol, hypertension, smoking, diabetes, obesity, inactivity, and family history.",
        "Heart-healthy diets limit saturated fat, trans fat, and sodium, and emphasize fruits, vegetables, whole grains, fish, and unsaturated oils. The DASH diet (Dietary Approaches to Stop Hypertension) lowers blood pressure. Heart failure may require sodium and fluid restriction.",
    ], [
        "Atherosclerosis = plaque in arteries",
        "Limit saturated fat, trans fat, sodium",
        "DASH diet lowers blood pressure",
        "Heart failure: restrict sodium and fluid",
    ]),
    (17, "Diet and Renal Disease", [
        "The kidneys filter waste, balance fluids and electrolytes, and regulate blood pressure. In kidney disease, waste products such as urea build up. Diet therapy may limit protein, sodium, potassium, phosphorus, and fluids depending on the stage.",
        "Dialysis removes wastes when kidneys fail; patients on dialysis often need more protein but continued limits on potassium, phosphorus, sodium, and fluid. Kidney stones may require increased fluids and diet changes based on stone type.",
    ], [
        "Kidney disease: limit protein, sodium, potassium, phosphorus, fluid",
        "Dialysis patients need more protein",
        "Drink plenty of fluids to prevent kidney stones",
        "Urea builds up when kidneys fail",
    ]),
    (18, "Diet and Gastrointestinal Disorders", [
        "Digestive disorders often need diet changes. GERD is helped by smaller meals, avoiding lying down after eating, and limiting fatty foods, caffeine, and alcohol. Peptic ulcers are usually caused by H. pylori or NSAIDs and are treated with medication.",
        "Diverticulosis is prevented with a high-fiber diet; during diverticulitis flares a low-fiber diet may be used. Celiac disease requires a strict gluten-free diet (no wheat, barley, or rye). Lactose intolerance is managed by limiting lactose. Crohn's disease and ulcerative colitis may need low-residue diets during flares. Liver and gallbladder disease may require fat restriction.",
    ], [
        "GERD: smaller meals; don't lie down after eating",
        "Celiac disease: gluten-free (no wheat, barley, rye)",
        "High fiber prevents diverticulosis",
        "Gallbladder disease: limit fat",
    ]),
    (19, "Diet and Cancer", [
        "Diet can affect cancer risk. A diet high in fruits, vegetables, and fiber and low in processed and red meat, alcohol, and excess calories lowers risk. Obesity increases the risk of several cancers.",
        "Cancer and its treatment (chemotherapy, radiation, surgery) can cause loss of appetite, nausea, taste changes, mouth sores, and weight loss (cachexia). Nutrition care focuses on small, frequent, high-calorie, high-protein meals and managing side effects.",
    ], [
        "Fruits, vegetables, fiber lower cancer risk",
        "Limit processed meat and alcohol",
        "Cachexia = severe wasting",
        "Small, frequent, high-calorie, high-protein meals during treatment",
    ]),
    (20, "Foodborne Illness, Allergies, and Intolerances", [
        "Foodborne illness is caused by pathogens such as Salmonella, E. coli, Listeria, Clostridium botulinum, and norovirus. Prevention follows four steps: clean (hands and surfaces), separate (avoid cross-contamination), cook to safe temperatures, and chill (refrigerate promptly). The danger zone for bacterial growth is 40–140°F.",
        "Food allergies are immune reactions; the most common allergens include milk, eggs, peanuts, tree nuts, fish, shellfish, wheat, soy, and sesame. Severe reactions (anaphylaxis) require epinephrine. Food intolerances, such as lactose intolerance, don't involve the immune system.",
    ], [
        "Clean, separate, cook, chill",
        "Danger zone: 40–140°F",
        "Allergy = immune reaction; intolerance is not",
        "Anaphylaxis requires epinephrine",
    ]),
    (21, "Nutritional Care of Clients with Special Needs", [
        "Some clients cannot eat normally and need special feeding methods. Enteral nutrition (tube feeding through a nasogastric, gastrostomy, or jejunostomy tube) is used when the GI tract works but the person can't eat enough. Parenteral nutrition delivers nutrients into a vein when the GI tract can't be used.",
        "Clients with burns, surgery, or infections need more calories and protein for healing. People with HIV/AIDS, dysphagia (difficulty swallowing, which may need thickened liquids), and other conditions require individualized nutrition care plans.",
    ], [
        "Enteral = tube feeding into the GI tract",
        "Parenteral = IV nutrition",
        "Burns and surgery raise calorie and protein needs",
        "Dysphagia may require thickened liquids",
    ]),
]

